Closes: issue #69

# Description
The Unscented Kalman Filter is a nonlinear state estimator. You use it to combine the outputs of a model of a system, and measurements of the system to estimate the true state over time.

More specifically, this pr implements a square-root Unscented Kalman Filter, where the lower-triangular square-root of the covariance matrix is maintained by the filter instead of a normal covariance matrix.

# Usage
This example is for a DC motor which simultaneously estimate position, velocity, kV, and kA:
Define the system model f(x, u), this is the derivative of your state with respect to time:
```cpp
auto f = [](const Eigen::Vector<double, 4>& xhat, const Eigen::Vector<double, 1>& u) -> Eigen::Vector<double, 4> {
    double v = xhat(1);
    double kV = xhat(2);
    double kA = xhat(3);
    double a = (u(0) - kV * v) / kA;
    Eigen::Vector<double, 4> xdot{v, a, 0, 0};
    return xdot;
};
```
Define the measurement function h(x, u), this computes the expected measurement at a given state:
```cpp
auto h = [](const Eigen::Vector<double, 4>& xhat, const Eigen::Vector<double, 1>& u) -> Eigen::Vector<double, 3> {
    double p = xhat(0);
    double v = xhat(1);
    double kV = xhat(2);
    double kA = xhat(3);
    double I = (u(0) - kV * v) / kA;
    Eigen::Vector<double, 3> z{p, v, I};
    return z;
};
```
Define the noise standard deviations for the system model and measurement model (noise is additive)
```cpp
Eigen::Vector<double, 4> f_stddevs{2.5e-4, 7e-2, 1e-10, 1e-10};
Eigen::Vector<double, 3> h_stddevs{0.02, 0.1, 0.1};
```
Create UKF object, we need to tell it which integration method to use, in this case RK4. The template parameters are <# states, # inputs, # measurement outputs>:
```cpp
SRUKF<4, 1, 3> srukf(f, h, RK4_with_input<4, 1>, f_stddevs, h_stddevs);
```

The filter has two functions that operate it, predict and correct. They do what they sound like they do, predict propagates the state and covariance forward by some timestep, and correct corrects the state and covariance by applying some measurement.

Usually these are called in pairs, unless you want to apply multiple measurements during the same timestep.
We assume here that there are vectors "inputs" and "measurements" already defined, these must be provided at each timestep.
In this example, we will say that the timestep is 10ms (update rate of VEX electronics)
```cpp
srukf.predict(inputs, 0.01);
srukf.correct(inputs, measurements);
```

To obtain the current state estimate, call:
```cpp
Eigen::Vector<double, 4> xhat = srukf.xhat();
```

# Testing

Various systems were tested (in simulation, not real life) to determine whether the state estimate converged correctly, to prove that the math is correct:
Four state system modeling a DC motor, [pos, vel, kV, kA]
Differential drive robot with LIDAR on an empty square field, [x, y, theta]
Glider with INS [x, y, z, vx, vy, vz]

Depending on the setup of the initial covariance matrix, and noise matrices, the filter converged for all these systems.

